---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const strengthCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Repetition maximum continuum', note: '1–5 reps heavy (strength + neural), 6–12 (hypertrophy sweet spot), 15+ (endurance) — the classic continuum, now refined: hypertrophy occurs across ALL loads to failure; heavy just does it with fewer reps and more rest.' },
    { math: '2 · Progressive overload, precisely', note: 'The training lesson\'s stress: muscles adapt, so stimulus must rise — via load, reps, sets or density (rest shortening). Progressive overload is the ONE non-negotiable principle; everything else is optimisation around it.' },
    { math: '3 · Neural before structural', note: 'First-month strength gains are mostly NERVOUS (better motor-unit recruitment, rate coding, coordination — the neuroscience lesson\'s motor units) — which is why beginners gain strength without visible size, and why the same lift improves fast.' },
    { math: '4 · Hypertrophy mechanics', note: 'Muscle grows from effective reps near fatigue, total volume (sets × reps × load) and mechanical tension — the "muscle confusion" myth dies here: muscles respond to PROGRESSIVE tension, not variety (though variety keeps joints and motivation healthy).' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/sports-science/intermediate/strength-and-hypertrophy">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Strength training is the training lesson\'s supercompensation, quantified for muscle and
    nerve: <strong>progressive overload drives everything; rep ranges and exercises are
    implementation details</strong>. The modern evidence simplified a gym\'s worth of folklore.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the continuum, overload, neural gains, hypertrophy" widget="equationStep" config={strengthCfg} />

  <h2>Programming essentials</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Compound movements are the base:</strong> squat, hinge, press, pull, carry — the patterns (not machines) train coordination and load the most muscle; the biomechanics lesson explains why a barbell squat beats any single-joint leg device.</li>
    <li><strong>Frequency 2×+/week per muscle beats once</strong> (protein synthesis elevated ~48 h post-session — the nutrition lesson\'s timing note) — the bro-split vs full-body debate resolved by biology.</li>
    <li><strong>Rest periods are load-specific:</strong> 2–5 min for heavy strength (phosphagen recovery — the energy lesson), 1–2 min for hypertrophy work; the "minimal rest for fat loss" habit sabotages load, and load is the stimulus.</li>
    <li><strong>Women and men respond to the same training:</strong> relative strength gains and hypertrophy per kg of muscle are comparable — program design differs by goal, not by myth.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Schoenfeld\'s hypertrophy meta-analyses; the strength-and-conditioning consensus statements; the training-load lesson.</p>
</TopicPage>
